---
import DocsLayout from "../../layouts/docs.astro";
---

<DocsLayout title="SQL Assist — Querial">
    <div class="space-y-8">
        <div>
            <p class="text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider text-querial-indigo-on-dark">For Developers</p>
            <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold mt-1">SQL Assist</h1>
        </div>
        <div class="prose dark:prose-invert max-w-none space-y-6 [&>p]:text-muted-foreground [&>h2]:text-foreground [&>h3]:text-foreground">
            <p>
                SQL Assist is a workspace that generates dialect SQL against a
                <strong class="text-foreground">schema source</strong> — a named,
                design-time catalog — using the instance LLM and embeddings
                configuration. It never auto-publishes. A human reviews, edits, and
                publishes. It is not free chat: <strong class="text-foreground">Send
                generates SQL</strong> from the selected source.
            </p>

            <h2>Schema source vs connection</h2>
            <p>
                A schema source is not a pipeline connection. It has one fetch secret
                and <em>no</em> environment kind. Include/exclude of SQL schemas is
                applied at fetch time, so Assist only sees in-scope objects. System
                schemas are always excluded. Cache, completion, and vector retrieval
                key off the schema source, not the runtime endpoint.
            </p>
            <p>
                <strong class="text-foreground">Run</strong> (from a SQL fence) uses a
                <strong class="text-foreground">logical connection × environment</strong>.
                Assist never executes SQL on the schema-source fetch string. Developers
                can run Development only. Operators may pick Staging or Production.
                If Send works but Run asks for a connection, pick a logical connection —
                that is expected.
            </p>
            <p>
                Binding a schema source on a draft step or migration is optional for
                publish. Execution must not depend on the cache being current. When
                both Connection and Schema are set, providers must match.
                <code class="text-foreground">artifact-sql</code> has no Schema control
                (DuckDB).
            </p>

            <h2>Who may refresh and index</h2>
            <p>
                This role lists enabled sources, browses the cache, and binds them.
                <strong class="text-foreground">Refresh, re-index, and cache delete
                are operator actions</strong> (DevOps / Project admin), on Schema
                Browser — not on the Assist page. Re-index rebuilds DBML and pgvector
                embeddings object by object. Platform Embedding config is required
                (the Default LLM is never used as an embedding fallback). A dimension
                mismatch against the control-database vector column fails closed.
            </p>

            <h2>The Assist workspace</h2>
            <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-1">
                <li>Full-canvas chat (left sidebar stays; no application top bar).</li>
                <li>Sessions stick to the schema source used when the conversation started.</li>
                <li>New session clears the canvas; a database row is created on first send.</li>
                <li>Replies stream. Stop cancels generation.</li>
                <li>User messages: Ask again / Copy / Delete. Assistant: Sources / Fullscreen / Copy markdown / Download / Delete.</li>
                <li>SQL fences: Copy, Run, Excel, Open in editor, fullscreen, plus Explain / Fix / Optimize for that fence. Fix uses the last run error, or asks for the error text.</li>
            </ul>
            <p>
                Run and Excel stay disabled until both a schema source and a connection
                are selected. The results modal shows at most 100 rows and can be
                stopped. Excel re-executes on the server (up to 50,000 rows; over that
                the export fails closed).
            </p>

            <h2>SQL Editor drawer</h2>
            <p>
                The SQL Editor has its own assistant drawer over Monaco — same sessions
                and generation. <strong class="text-foreground">Apply to editor</strong>
                writes a SQL fence into the buffer. It is not a second copy of the
                Assist page. Fence Run / Excel still use the connection × environment
                already selected on the editor toolbar.
            </p>

            <h2>Generate SQL in the DAG</h2>
            <p>
                Step and migration modals have <strong class="text-foreground">Generate
                SQL</strong>. That uses the step’s bound schema source (Artifact SQL
                does not bind one) and inserts a <strong class="text-foreground">draft</strong>
                into Monaco. It does not auto-save and does not publish. Physical
                staging names (<code class="text-foreground">_querial_stg_*</code>)
                never survive into the inserted text. For artifact sinks, assistance
                must emit logical tokens:
            </p>
            <pre class="rounded-xl border border-border/50 bg-muted/40 p-4 text-sm overflow-x-auto"><code>{"{{ input }}"}                 -- Artifact SQL, one inbound file
{"{{ input.sales_order_transformed }}"}  -- Artifact SQL, several files (producer outputName)
{"{{ stage }}"}                 -- Staged Database SQL, one inbound file
{"{{ stage.sales_order }}"}     -- Staged Database SQL, several files</code></pre>
            <p>
                Review the draft, bind Connection, then save. Pipeline execution still
                uses the published version — never the chat history.
            </p>

            <h2>Secrets and prompts</h2>
            <p>
                Fetch strings, connection secrets, and API keys never appear in prompts,
                logs, or vector payloads. Embeddings and chat history live on the
                control database (PostgreSQL 18 with pgvector). Platform admin owns
                LLM and embedding configuration; this role does not.
            </p>
            <p>
                Interactive SQL environment rules:
                <a href="/docs/developer-sql" class="text-link hover:underline">SQL workspace, steps, and migrations</a>.
                Schema source stubs in packages:
                <a href="/docs/developer-packages" class="text-link hover:underline">Packages</a>.
                Tokens and Fill:
                <a href="/docs/developer-handler-contracts" class="text-link hover:underline">Step handler contracts</a>.
            </p>
        </div>
    </div>
</DocsLayout>
